.TH CHANGELOG-FRAGMENT 5 "July 2026" "changelog 1.0.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
changelog fragment \- YAML fragment file format for changelog entries
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Fragment files are the primary unit of changelog content in this tool. Each fragment describes a single change (bugfix, enhancement, deprecation, etc.) with metadata including issue reference, category, date, and human-readable title.

Fragments accumulate over time as developers add them via \fBchangelog add\fR, then get aggregated into release notes by \fBchangelog release\fR or \fBchangelog generate\fR.
.SH FILE LOCATION
.PP
By default, fragments are stored in:
.IP \(bu 2
\fIchangelogs/fragments/\fR \- Main fragment directory

.TP
.B Directory Structure:
.nf
    changelogs/
        config.yaml          # Project configuration
        fragments/           # Fragment files directory
            PG-001-bugfixes.yml
            IMP-456-enhancement.yml
            DOC-123-deprecation.yml
.fi

.TP
.B Alternative Locations:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
\fINotes directories\fR specified via \fB\-\-notes-dir\fR in release/generate commands
.IP \(bu 2
\fICustom fragment paths\fR configured in changelog.yaml under notes_dirs setting
.RE

.SH FILE NAMING CONVENTION
.PP
Fragment filenames follow this pattern:
.nf
    <ISSUE-ID>-<CATEGORY>.yml
.fi

.TP
.B Components:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
\fI<ISSUE\-ID>\fR \- Unique identifier (e.g., "PG\-001", "BUG\-12345", "DOC\-789")
.IP \(bu 2
\fI<CATEGORY>\fR \- Change category matching one of the supported types (see below)
.IP \(bu 2
\fI.yml\fR extension \- YAML format for proper parsing and tooling support
.RE

.TP
.B Category Values:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
\fIbugfixes\fR, \fImajor_changes\fR, \fiminor_changes\fR, \fIbreaking_changes\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fIdeprecated_features\fR, \fIremoved_features\fR, \fIsecurity_fixes\fR, \fIknown_issues\fR
.RE

.SH YAML STRUCTURE
.PP
Each fragment file contains a single YAML mapping with category as the top-level key:

.nf
    <category>:
      - text: |
          Fragment body content here...
        title: "Optional human-readable title"
        date: "YYYY-MM-DD"  # Optional, defaults to today
.fi

.TP
.B Example Fragment (PG\-001-bugfixes.yml):
.nf
    bugfixes:
      - text: |
          Fixed crash when processing large files.
          The application would hang indefinitely on inputs over 1GB.
        title: "Stability improvement for large file handling"
        date: "2026-07-15"
.fi

.TP
.B Example Fragment with Multiple Entries (IMP\-456-enhancement.yml):
.nf
    minor_changes:
      - text: |
          Added support for parallel processing of multiple files.
          Performance improved by 10x on multi-core systems.
        title: "Parallel file processing capability"

      - text: |
          New configuration option --max-threads controls concurrency level.
        title: "Thread count configuration"
.fi

.SH REQUIRED FIELDS
.PP
While the tool accepts fragments with minimal structure, the following fields are recommended for optimal processing:

.TP
.B Required (for release generation):
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
\fItext\fR \- Fragment body content (markdown format)
.IP \(bu 2
\fItitle\fR \- Human-readable summary of the change
.RE

.TP
.B Optional but Recommended:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
\fIdate\fR \- Date in YYYY-MM-DD format for chronological sorting
.IP \(bu 2
\fImeta\fR \- Additional metadata (author, issue_type, etc.)
.RE

.SH OPTIONAL FIELDS
.PP
Fragments can include additional metadata fields that influence processing and display:

.TP
.B Metadata Fields:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
\fIauthor\fR \- Person who created the fragment (email or name)
.IP \(bu 2
\fIissue_type\fR \- Classification of change (bug, feature, docs, etc.)
.IP \(bu 2
\fItags\fR \- Array of classification tags for filtering and grouping
.IP \(bu 2
\fIscope\fR \- Component or module affected by the change
.RE

.TP
.B Example with Metadata:
.nf
    bugfixes:
      - text: "Fixed null pointer exception in parser"
        title: "Parser stability fix"
        date: "2026-07-20"
        meta:
          author: "developer@example.com"
          issue_type: "bug"
          scope: "core/parser"
.fi

.SH SPECIAL CHARACTERS AND QUOTING
.PP
Fragment content supports standard YAML quoting rules. Use double quotes for strings containing special characters:

.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
\fIColons in text:\fR Quote with double quotes to avoid parsing as dict keys
.IP \(bu 2
\fINewlines:\fR Use block scalars (|) or folded scalars (>) for multi-line content
.IP \(bu 2
\fISpecial characters:\fR Escape with backslash or use quoted strings
.RE

.TP
.B Correct Quoting Examples:
.nf
    # Bad - colon causes YAML parsing as dict
    text: "Fixed issue: crash on large files"

    # Good - properly quoted string
    text: 'Fixed issue: crash on large files'

    # Good - block scalar for multi-line content
    text: |
      Fixed crash when processing large files.
      The application would hang indefinitely on inputs over 1GB.
.fi

.SH VALIDATION RULES
.PP
Fragments are validated during \fBchangelog lint\fR and release generation:

.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
YAML syntax must be valid (parseable by standard YAML libraries)
.IP \(bu 2
Category key must match one of the supported values
.IP \(bu 2
Issue ID in filename should be unique within the fragments directory
.IP \(bu 2
Text content should not be empty or whitespace-only
.RE

.TP
.B Strict Validation (\fBchangelog lint --strict\fR):
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
Date field required and must be in YYYY-MM-DD format
.IP \(bu 2
Link syntax validated for proper URL formatting
.IP \(bu 2
No colon-in-string issues that break YAML parsing
.RE

.SH SEE ALSO
.TP
\fBchangelog(8)\fR \- Main changelog command with fragment workflow overview
.TP
\fBchangelog-add(8)\fR \- Create new fragment files (documents fragment creation process)
.TP
\fBchangelog-lint(8)\fR \- Validate fragments for correctness and consistency
